.TH IO_NEW 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
iob_new \- Create a new I/O batch buffer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/iob_internal.h>

.IB io_batch*
.IB iob_new(
.IB int hint_entries
.IB );

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBiob_new\fR function creates a new I/O batch buffer. This function is used to allocate and initialize an I/O batch buffer, which can then be used to manage I/O operations in batch mode.

The function takes the following parameter:

.IB hint_entries
Hint for the number of entries in the batch buffer. This is used to pre-allocate memory, which can improve performance by reducing the number of memory allocations during batch processing.

The function returns a pointer to the newly created I/O batch buffer, or NULL if the allocation fails.

The function is part of the \fBiob\fR library, which provides a framework for managing I/O operations in batch mode.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBiob_new\fR function to create a new I/O batch buffer:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/iob.h>

int main() {
  io_batch* batch = iob_new(100); // Hint for 100 entries
  if (!batch) {
    perror("iob_new");
    return 1;
  }

  // Add data to the batch buffer
  iob_adds(batch, "Hello, World!");
  iob_adds(batch, "This is a test.");

  // Process the batch buffer
  while (iob_bytesleft(batch) > 0) {
    iob_execute(batch);
  }

  // Free the I/O batch buffer
  iob_free(batch);

  printf("I/O batch buffer created, processed, and freed\n");

  return 0;
}
.nef